      "url": "https://memcyco.com/disrupting-ai-powered-phishing-what-cisos-must-do-now/",
      "description": "Disrupting AI-Powered Phishing: What CISOs Must Do Now Skip to content secures $37M Series A to preempt Digital Impersonation & ATO scams 🎉 Watch the vid Learn More Research: why fraud prevention must start before login Get the report Main Menu Solutions Solutions Overview Solutions overview By use case Account takeover (ATO) Digital Impersonation Credit card data theft By attack technique Man in the Middle (MitM) Remote desktop takeover Credential stuffing SEO poisoning By team For security teams For fraud & risk teams For digital business teams By industry Banking Credit Unions Airlines Retail & eCommerce Hospitality Products Technology Resources Library Blog Glossary The Attack Playbook Frost & Sullivan Analysis of Memcyco Read the article Disrupting AI-Powered Phishing: What CISOs Must Do Now Read the article Memcyco Fraud & ATO Glossary Explore the Glossary Inside the Attack Account Takeover (ATO) Man-in-the-Middle Remote Access Credential Stuffing Partnerships Partner Up Partner Portal Company About News Events Careers Contact Book a demo Solutions Overview Solutions overview By use case Account Takeover (ATO) Digital Impersonation Credit card data theft By attack technique Man in the Middle (MitM) Remote desktop takeover Credential stuffing SEO poisoning By team For security teams For fraud/risk teams For digital business teams By industry Banking Credit Unions Airlines Retail & Ecommerce Hospitality Products Technology Resources Resources Library Blog Glossary The Attack Playbook Account Takeover (ATO) Man-in-the-Middle Remote Desktop Takeover Credential Stuffing Partners Partnerships Partner Up Partner Portal Company Company About News Events Careers Contact Memcyco Blog Get the latest insights and protect your business and your customers from website spoofing fraud. Fraud Detection Disrupting AI-Powered Phishing: What CISOs Must Do Now By Ken Palla May 9, 2025 Reading time: 4 Min It’s all over the news: GenAI will fuel a rise in scams and fraud. That’s a big claim, so let’s unpack what it means through the lens of one threat vector in particular: phishing . Strategic Briefing: Moving Upstream of the Inbox The Detection Gap: Traditional phishing defense starts at the inbox. In reality, 60% of phishing domains receive a TLS certificate within two hours of registration. Attackers are live and ready before the first email is even sent. The Awareness Limit: While training is valuable, it is not a technical control. Even the best-trained users have a 21-second median click time under pressure. Relying on “Human Detection” means your first signal of an attack is a victim who has already clicked. The “Lifecycle” Reframe: Effective detection must move to Stages 1 & 2 (Domain Registration & Infrastructure Setup). Detecting visual clones and lookalike patterns before distribution shrinks the average 21-hour exposure window to nearly zero. Maturity Metric (PCDR): The ultimate KPI for a modern SOC is the Pre-Compromise Detection Rate. If you aren’t catching at least 50% of campaigns before they reach a user’s inbox, your detection posture is structurally reactive. Shock Phishing News (That’s No Longer Shocking) Security leaders subscribed to any major cybersecurtity publication may have encountered some of these stories. Sadly, these scenarios are so common that they’re unsurprising. That desensitization is part of the problem because it drives a tired acceptance of the problem as inevitable. It’s no wonder so many businesses For the sake of example, let’s pick out some of the most alarming findings around AI-powered phishing. AI-Powered Spear Phishing Research (November 2024) Research by Hoxhunt and others in late 2024 found that AI-powered spear phishing tools can automate the entire attack process: gathering information, profiling targets, and generating messages. By November 2024, AI-generated spear phishing attacks performed nearly on par with human experts, and by early 2025, even surpassed them in effectiveness. Source FBI Warnings on GenAI and Fraud (December 2024) In December 2024 , the FBI issued a warning about GenAI in fraud. Two key callouts for phishing: “Criminals use generative AI tools to assist with language translations to limit grammatical or spelling errors for foreign criminal actors targeting US victims.” “Criminals create messages to send to victims faster, allowing them to reach a wider audience with believable content.” You get the picture and there’s no end of examples I could reference. Most of this is news to few, but all of it is of acute relevance to anyone whose job is it to keep scammers out of customer accounts. The bottom line is that AI is industrializing highly personalized, seemingly credible phishing at scale – and the bad actors behind AI-assisted scams are getting younger, with zero technical expertise. No matter where you’re sitting, if you’re responsible for customer account security, that’s a huge problem – one that won’t solve itself. AI-powered phishing isn’t a trend, it’s a defining feature in the new threat landscape. Ok, point made. To set up the narrative, let’s go back to basics. Phishing’s Primary Goal: Credential Theft and Malware Drops Everyone reading this knows that phishing typically aims to: Trick users into entering credentials on a fake website Trick users into downloading malicious code Here we’ll focus on the first, since it’s the main driver behind the monumental surge in account takeover (ATO) . Why Credential Theft Is Still in Fashion Despite increased digital security spending, most user authentication still hinges on weak factors: A password A one-time passcode (OTP) Both can be phished. Today’s fraudsters don’t just trick users into entering credentials, they follow up with real-time social engineering to intercept OTPs. Some even use OTP phishing kits-as-a-service, making full credential theft scalable and repeatable. That’s how phishing leads directly to account takeover. The Modern Attack Chain Let’s break it down: Step",
