he users and accounts exposed to the risk. The MemcycoFM Show An AI-Generated Podcast Why You Should Listen This episode breaks down why discovering a fake site is not enough when teams can't tell which users encountered it, submitted credentials, or may need attention before account takeover risk develops. Do you know where an impersonation campaign is running but not which customers it reached? This episode helps you spot the exposure gap and act on it earlier. READ ACCOMPANYING BLOG POST Key Takeouts You will see how exposure identification connects interactions with impersonating assets to credential-risk indicators, device context, and authentication-stage signals, giving teams a clearer view of which users and accounts may require closer attention. For security, fraud, identity, and CISO-level leaders, this is a current operational concern: knowing a fake domain exists is not the same as knowing which customers may now face credential or account takeover risk. Why brand impersonation is so effective A fake site, search result, SMS link, social profile, or login experience can intercept users who believe they are engaging with a trusted brand. Exposure can create the conditions for credential harvesting, device risk, account probing, and downstream fraud. Why traditional detection falls short Asset discovery, takedowns, blocklists, and customer reports can reveal an impersonation campaign, but they don't automatically identify who interacted with it. By the time signals are connected, the organization may already be investigating loss rather than preventing it. What real-time detection changes Memcyco connects supported brand impersonation attacks to exposed users, credential-risk indicators, device context, and authentication-stage signals. This gives teams earlier, actionable intelligence to prioritize at-risk accounts and intervene before exposure becomes account takeover. Listen to the full podcast episode below. Solutions Overview Solution overview By use 